<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - Review Problems #1, practice version.                          -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Rebuilt from the WeBWorK set: every answer box is graded on its own, so  -->
<!-- partial credit survives, and the feedback is a bare Correct./Incorrect.  -->
<!-- Each <solution> carries component="review1-solutions" so the set can be   -->
<!-- posted from publication/publication.ptx after the due date.              -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- WHY THE SOLUTIONS LOOK ODD.  Each <solution> is a bare element and it    -->
<!-- is its *children* that carry component="review1-solutions"; the element   -->
<!-- itself is never dropped, and it carries include-automatic="no".         -->
<!-- Both details matter.  These are dynamic "fillin" exercises, and when     -->
<!-- such an exercise has no authored solution marked include-automatic="no", -->
<!-- PreTeXt manufactures one by copying the whole statement with the answers -->
<!-- filled in.  That copy duplicates every xml:id and every PreFigure @label -->
<!-- inside the statement - and the figures live inside these statements - so -->
<!-- asset generation reports duplicate identifiers and the build fails.      -->
<!-- Keeping the solution element present, with its contents gated instead,   -->
<!-- suppresses the manufactured copy whether or not solutions are posted.    -->
<!-- assets/unnumbered-chapter.css hides the empty "Solution." heading that   -->
<!-- remains while the contents are switched off.                            -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-review1-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Review Problems #1</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="sec-parametrization" text="title"/>,
      <xref ref="sec-arc-length" text="title"/>, and
      <xref ref="sec-line-integrals" text="title"/>. Work each problem, then
      type your answers into the boxes and have them checked here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term> Type numbers as decimals to at
      least three places, with no units and no <m>\pi</m>. Type expressions
      with <c>*</c> for products and <c>^</c> for powers, and write functions
      with parentheses: <c>cos(t)</c>, <c>sqrt(x)</c>, <c>e^t</c>,
      <c>ln(s)</c>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-review1-practice">

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-cycloid-speed">
    <title>Speed Where the Tangent Line is Horizontal</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="speed" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*a</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The position of an object at time <m>t</m> is given by
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle <eval obj="a"/>t
        - <eval obj="a"/>\sin t,\ <eval obj="a"/>
        - <eval obj="a"/>\cos t \right\rangle .
      </me>
      <p>
        Find its speed at the points where the tangent line is horizontal.
      </p>
      <p>
        speed <m>=</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="speed" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="speed.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(speed.value({})))" max="speed.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(speed.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The slope of the tangent line is
        <m>\dfrac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}
        = \dfrac{a\sin t}{a - a\cos t} = \dfrac{\sin t}{1-\cos t}</m>,
        which vanishes when <m>\sin t = 0</m> and <m>\cos t \ne 1</m>, that
        is at <m>t = \pi(2k-1)</m>. The speed is
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        \left\lVert\mathbf{v}\right\rVert
        = \sqrt{\left(a - a\cos t\right)^2 + \left(a\sin t\right)^2}
        = \sqrt{2a^2\left(1-\cos t\right)}
        = 2a\left\lvert\sin\tfrac{t}{2}\right\rvert ,
      </me>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        and at <m>t = \pi(2k-1)</m> the sine equals <m>\pm 1</m>, so the
        speed is <m>2a</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-cartesian-from-parametric">
    <title>A Cartesian Equation and a Tangent Line</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="7" max="11" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="poly" context="formula">
        <de-expression>b^3*x^3+a^3*y^3-a^2*b^2*x*y</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="tangent" context="formula">
        <de-expression>(-b/a)*(x-a/2)+b/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find a Cartesian equation relating <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> for the
        parametric curve
      </p>
      <me>
        x = \frac{<eval obj="a"/>t}{1+t^3}, \qquad
        y = \frac{<eval obj="b"/>t^2}{1+t^3}, \qquad t \ne -1 .
      </me>
      <p>
        Write your answer in the form <m>P(x,y) = 0</m>, where
        <m>P(x,y)</m> is a polynomial in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> whose
        <m>x^3</m> coefficient is <m><eval obj="b"/>^3</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>P(x,y) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="poly" width="30"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        Find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point
        corresponding to <m>t = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="tangent" width="30"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        Write <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> for the two coefficients. Then
        <m>x^3 = \dfrac{a^3t^3}{(1+t^3)^3}</m> and
        <m>y^3 = \dfrac{b^3t^6}{(1+t^3)^3}</m>, while
        <m>xy = \dfrac{ab\,t^3}{(1+t^3)^2}</m>. Substituting into
        <m>b^3x^3 + a^3y^3 - a^2b^2xy</m> and putting everything over
        <m>(1+t^3)^3</m>, the numerator is
        <m>a^3b^3t^3\left(1 + t^3 - (1+t^3)\right) = 0</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        P(x,y) = b^3x^3 + a^3y^3 - a^2b^2xy = 0 .
      </me>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        At <m>t = 1</m> the point is
        <m>\left(\tfrac{a}{2}, \tfrac{b}{2}\right)</m>, and
        <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \dfrac{y'(1)}{x'(1)} = -\dfrac{b}{a}</m>, so the
        tangent line is
        <m>y = -\dfrac{b}{a}\left(x - \dfrac{a}{2}\right) + \dfrac{b}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-helix-scalar">
    <title>A Scalar Line Integral Along a Helix</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="n" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>sqrt(2)*(a*pi+pi^(n+1)/(n+1))</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the integral of
        <m>f(x,y,z) = <eval obj="a"/>x^2 + <eval obj="a"/>y^2
        + z^{<eval obj="n"/>}</m> over the curve
        <m>\mathbf{c}(t) = \left(\cos t,\ \sin t,\ t\right)</m> for
        <m>0 \le t \le \pi</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{\mathcal{C}} f\,ds =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="value" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="value.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))" max="value.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        Since <m>\mathbf{c}'(t) = \langle -\sin t, \cos t, 1\rangle</m>, we
        have <m>\lVert\mathbf{c}'(t)\rVert = \sqrt2</m> and
        <m>ds = \sqrt2\,dt</m>. On the curve
        <m>a\cos^2 t + a\sin^2 t + t^{n} = a + t^{n}</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        \int_{\mathcal{C}} f\,ds
        = \sqrt2\int_0^{\pi}\left(a + t^{n}\right)dt
        = \sqrt2\left(a\pi + \frac{\pi^{\,n+1}}{n+1}\right) .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-exponential-path">
    <title>A Vector Line Integral Along an Exponential Path</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="T" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*(exp(2*T)-exp(-2*T))-(exp(T)-exp(-T))</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the line integral of the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle 3zy^{-1},\ 4x,\ -y \right\rangle</m>
        over the path
        <m>\mathbf{c}(t) = \left\langle e^t, e^t, t\right\rangle</m> for
        <m>-<eval obj="T"/> \le t \le <eval obj="T"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{\mathcal{C}} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="value" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="value.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))" max="value.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        On the path, <m>\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{c}(t))
        = \left\langle 3te^{-t},\ 4e^{t},\ -e^{t}\right\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{c}'(t) = \left\langle e^{t}, e^{t}, 1\right\rangle</m>,
        so the integrand is
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        \mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{c}'(t) = 3t + 4e^{2t} - e^{t} .
      </me>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The term <m>3t</m> is odd and integrates to <m>0</m> over the
        symmetric interval, so
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        \int_{\mathcal{C}} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \Big[2e^{2t} - e^{t}\Big]_{-T}^{T}
        = 2\left(e^{2T} - e^{-2T}\right) - \left(e^{T} - e^{-T}\right) .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-circle-in-plane">
    <title>A Circle Traced in Space</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="3" max="13" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The function <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> traces a circle. Determine the
        plane containing it, its center, and its radius.
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}(t) = <eval obj="c"/>\,\mathbf{i}
        + \left(<eval obj="R"/>\cos t\right)\mathbf{j}
        + \left(<eval obj="R"/>\sin t\right)\mathbf{k}
      </me>
      <p>
        Plane: <m>x =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="c" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        Center: <m>\Big(</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="c" width="6"/>,
        <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="6"/>,
        <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="zero" width="6"/>
        <m>\Big)</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Radius: <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="R" width="6"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="c.value({})-0.01" max="c.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="c.value({})-0.01" max="c.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-0.01" max="zero.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="zero.value({})-0.01" max="zero.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="R.value({})-0.01" max="R.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        Here <m>x(t) = c</m> is constant, so the curve lies in the vertical
        plane <m>x = c</m>. In that plane,
        <m>y(t)^2 + z(t)^2 = R^2\cos^2 t + R^2\sin^2 t = R^2</m>, the
        equation of a circle of radius <m>R</m> centered at
        <m>(c, 0, 0)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-speed-distance">
    <title>Time, Distance and Speed of a Moving Object</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="gap" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="5" max="11" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>a+gap</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="20" max="25" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="L" context="number">
        <de-expression>b^3/3+b-a^3/3-a</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="sp" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2+1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The position vector of an object is
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}(t) = \frac{t^2}{\sqrt2}\cos\!\left(\sqrt2\,t\right)
        \mathbf{i} + \frac{t^2}{\sqrt2}\sin\!\left(\sqrt2\,t\right)
        \mathbf{j} + \left(<eval obj="c"/> - t\right)\mathbf{k} .
      </me>
      <p>
        Calculate the time <m>t</m> it takes the object to reach
        <m>z = 0</m>; the distance <m>L</m> it travels from
        <m>t = <eval obj="a"/></m> until <m>t = <eval obj="b"/></m>; and its
        speed at <m>t = <eval obj="a"/></m> seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>t =</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="c" width="8"/>
        seconds
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>L =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="L" width="12"/>
        meters
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{v}\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)\right\rVert =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="sp" width="10"/>
        meters per second
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="c.value({})-0.01" max="c.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="L.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(L.value({})))" max="L.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(L.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="sp.value({})-0.01" max="sp.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The <m>z</m>-coordinate is <m>c - t</m>, so <m>z = 0</m> at
        <m>t = c</m>. Differentiating and simplifying,
        <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert = t^2 + 1</m>, so the speed
        at <m>t = a</m> is <m>a^2 + 1</m>, and the distance travelled is
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        L = \int_a^b \left(t^2+1\right)dt
        = \left(\frac{b^3}{3} + b\right) - \left(\frac{a^3}{3} + a\right) .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-work-parabola">
    <title>Work Along a Parabola</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="work" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*7/3+(exp(2)-exp(1))/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate the work done by the force field
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = <eval obj="a"/>x^2\,\mathbf{i}
        + ye^{x}\,\mathbf{j}</m> on a particle that moves along the curve
        <m>C: x = y^2+1</m> from <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>(2,1)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Work <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="work" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="work.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(work.value({})))" max="work.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(work.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        Parametrize by <m>y = t</m>, <m>x = t^2+1</m>, <m>0 \le t \le 1</m>,
        so <m>dx = 2t\,dt</m> and <m>dy = dt</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        W = \int_0^1 \left[a\left(t^2+1\right)^2(2t)
        + t\,e^{t^2+1}\right]dt
        = \frac{7a}{3} + \frac{e^2 - e}{2} .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-spiral-length">
    <title>The Length of an Involute</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="len" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the length of the parametrized curve
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}(\theta) = \left\langle \sin\theta - \theta\cos\theta,\
        \cos\theta + \theta\sin\theta \right\rangle, \qquad
        0 \le \theta \le <eval obj="a"/> .
      </me>
      <p>
        Length <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="len" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="len.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(len.value({})))" max="len.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(len.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        Differentiating, <m>x' = \theta\sin\theta</m> and
        <m>y' = \theta\cos\theta</m>, so
        <m>\sqrt{x'^2 + y'^2} = \theta</m> for <m>\theta \ge 0</m> and
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        S = \int_0^{a} \theta\,d\theta = \frac{a^2}{2} .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-sign-of-circulation">
    <title>The Sign of a Line Integral Around a Closed Curve</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the closed curve <m>C</m> shown below. For each vector
        field, decide whether
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> is
        positive, negative, or zero. Type <c>positive</c>, <c>negative</c>
        or <c>zero</c>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j}</m>:
        <fillin name="b1" mode="string" answer="zero" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F} = -y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m>:
        <fillin name="b2" mode="string" answer="positive" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F} = y\,\mathbf{i} - x\,\mathbf{j}</m>:
        <fillin name="b3" mode="string" answer="negative" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-r1p-annulus">
        <caption>
          The closed curve <m>C</m>, with the direction of travel marked on
          the two straight edges.
        </caption>
        <image width="62%">
          <shortdescription>A closed curve made of two circular arcs of radius one and two between minus forty-five and plus forty-five degrees, joined by two radial segments, with arrows on the straight edges showing the direction of travel.</shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-r1p-annulus">
            <diagram dimensions="(330,330)" margins="8">
              <definition>fInner(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
              <definition>fOuter(t) = (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-0.65,-1.75,2.85,1.75)">
                <axes at="ax" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1" stroke="#555555" thickness="1"/>
                <parametric-curve at="inner" function="fInner" domain="(-pi/4,pi/4)" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
                <parametric-curve at="outer" function="fOuter" domain="(-pi/4,pi/4)" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
                <line at="lower" endpoints="((0.7071,-0.7071),(1.4142,-1.4142))" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
                <line at="upper" endpoints="((1.4142,1.4142),(0.7071,0.7071))" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
                <vector at="a-lower" v="(0.2404,-0.2404)" tail="(1.0041,-1.0041)" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
                <vector at="a-upper" v="(-0.2404,-0.2404)" tail="(1.1172,1.1172)" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
                <label at="lab" anchor="(1.5,0.30)" alignment="c"><m>C</m></label>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">zero</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">positive</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">negative</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The curve bounds the annular sector between <m>r=1</m> and
        <m>r=2</m> from <m>-45^\circ</m> to <m>45^\circ</m>, traversed with
        the region on the left, so it is positively oriented. Its area is
        <m>\tfrac12\left(\tfrac{\pi}{2}\right)\left(2^2-1^2\right)
        = \tfrac{3\pi}{4}</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The first field is radial: perpendicular to both arcs, and parallel
        to the two straight edges, which are travelled once outward and once
        inward, so those contributions cancel and the integral is <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        For the second, <m>N_x - M_y = 2</m>, so Green's Theorem gives
        <m>2\cdot\text{Area} = \tfrac{3\pi}{2}</m>, which is positive.
        The third is its negative, <m>-\tfrac{3\pi}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-match-fields">
    <title>Matching Vector Fields to Their Graphs</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Match each of the following vector fields with its graph, entering
        the letter <c>A</c>, <c>B</c>, <c>C</c>, <c>D</c> or <c>E</c>. The
        graphs are shown below; in each one a small circle marks the
        <term>tail</term> of the arrow.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \dfrac{x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j}}
        {\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m>:
        <fillin name="b1" mode="string" answer="C" width="4"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \dfrac{-y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}}
        {\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m>:
        <fillin name="b2" mode="string" answer="D" width="4"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \dfrac{-x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j}}
        {\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m>:
        <fillin name="b3" mode="string" answer="A" width="4"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \dfrac{x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j}}
        {x^2+y^2}</m>:
        <fillin name="b4" mode="string" answer="E" width="4"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \dfrac{-x\,\mathbf{i} - y\,\mathbf{j}}
        {x^2+y^2}</m>:
        <fillin name="b5" mode="string" answer="B" width="4"/>
      </p>
