        <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">C</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">D</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">A</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">E</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">B</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The curve bounds the annular sector between <m>r=1</m> and
        <m>r=2</m> from <m>-45^\circ</m> to <m>45^\circ</m>, and the
        arrows traverse it with the region on the left, so it is positively
        oriented. Its area is
        <m>\tfrac12\left(\tfrac{\pi}{2}\right)\left(2^2-1^2\right)
        = \tfrac{3\pi}{4}</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The first field is radial, so it is everywhere perpendicular to the
        two arcs and everywhere parallel to the two radial segments, which
        are traversed once outward and once inward: the contributions cancel
        and the integral is <m>0</m>. (It is a gradient field,
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \nabla\!\left(\tfrac12(x^2+y^2)\right)</m>, and
        <m>C</m> is closed.)
      </p>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        For the second, <m>N_x - M_y = 1-(-1) = 2</m>, so by Green's Theorem
        the circulation is <m>2\cdot\text{Area} = \tfrac{3\pi}{2} \gt 0</m>:
        the field circles counterclockwise, the same way as <m>C</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The third is the negative of the second, so its circulation is
        <m>-\tfrac{3\pi}{2} \lt 0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r1p-order-integrals">
    <title>Ordering Three Line Integrals</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> shown in
        <xref ref="fig-r1p-three-paths"/> together with the paths
        <m>C_1</m>, <m>C_2</m> and <m>C_3</m>. Arrange the three line
        integrals
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{C_1} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>,
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{C_2} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> and
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{C_3} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> in
        ascending order. Type <c>C1</c>, <c>C2</c> or <c>C3</c> in each box.
      </p>
      <p>
        smallest: <fillin name="b1" mode="string" answer="C2" width="6"/>
        <m>\lt</m>
        middle: <fillin name="b2" mode="string" answer="C3" width="6"/>
        <m>\lt</m>
        largest: <fillin name="b3" mode="string" answer="C1" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-r1p-three-paths">
        <caption>
          The vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the three paths
          <m>C_1</m>, <m>C_2</m> and <m>C_3</m>. Each field vector is drawn with
          a dot at its tail.
