    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">C2</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">C3</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">C1</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        The field is constant and horizontal,
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \mathbf{i}</m>, so
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = dx</m> and each integral is
        simply the net horizontal displacement of its path.
      </p>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        <m>C_2</m> is vertical, so it gains no <m>x</m> at all and its
        integral is <m>0</m>. <m>C_3</m> moves <m>0.75</m> to the right,
        and <m>C_1</m> moves <m>1.5</m> to the right, in the direction of
        the field. Hence
      </p>
      <me component="review1-solutions">
        \int_{C_2} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0
        \lt \int_{C_3} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0.75
        \lt \int_{C_1} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = 1.5 .
      </me>
      <p component="review1-solutions">
        Only the horizontal displacement matters; the vertical motion of
        <m>C_3</m> contributes nothing.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
